A stack is a linear data structure (like an array) where both insertion and deletion of an element
take place at the same end, known as the top. New elements are added (pushed) at the top, and
existing elements can only be removed (popped) from the top. This follows the LIFO (Last In, First Out)
rule, where the most recently added item is removed first. You can easily implement this behavior
using a 1-D array by using a variable like top to track the index of the last inserted element. A code that
implements is partially given below. You complete the code by writing three functions: push(), pop(),
and display() to show the elements of the stack. [15]
